#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# catch

#% text
# `catch(e)` evaluates `e` (held) and gives the value `v` of the first `throw(v)` reached in it, or the value of `e` when nothing was thrown. `catch(e, form)` catches only throws `throw(v, tag)` whose tag matches the pattern `form`, and `catch(e, form, f)` gives `f(v, tag)`. A throw that does not match stays a failure and goes on to an outer `catch`.
#
# A throw is a failure value (`failure(thrown, ...)`), so the first throw reached is the first one in evaluation order: inside a list or the result of `map`, where failures are kept, `catch` takes the leftmost.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
catch({ a; b; throw(c); d })
#% output
#= c

#% cell
catch(for k in range(100) { if k^2 > 50 then throw(k) })
#% output
#= 8

#% cell
catch(map(x => if x < 0 then throw(x) else x, [1, 2, -3, 4, -5]))
#% output
#= -3

#% cell
catch([catch([a, throw(b), c]), d])
#% output
#= [b, d]

#% cell
catch(catch(throw(1, small) + 2, big), small)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
catch(throw(10, overflow), _, (v, t) => [t, v])
#% output
#= [overflow, 10]

